Note (honest): the value baselines attain high value-ROC but pathological operating
points (e.g. iforest-corrected flags a large benign fraction at 'auto' contamination):
in the common USD denomination, benign whale transfers are of the same order as the
largest drain rows (only the single biggest Ronin leg exceeds every benign transfer,
and the Nomad rows sit well inside the benign bulk) — no per-tx value
threshold separates incidents on value alone. The sequential detector's contribution is
the low-and-slow recall + near-zero phi below, not the raw per-row AUC (a streaming
change-point detector is not a per-row population classifier).

Note (V2 2.4, published-rules comparator — read before comparing rows): the
xchainwatcher-rules row re-implements XChainWatcher's core cross-chain soundness rules
(Augusto et al., arXiv:2410.02029) over the parquet's deposit<->withdrawal linkage
columns. TWO caveats: (a) the CCTX incident labels were DERIVED BY XChainWatcher's
unmatched-withdrawal analysis, so its ~perfect incident recall here is BY CONSTRUCTION
— this is a capability/deployment-cost comparison (both chains instrumented +
protocol-specific rules vs BOLT's destination-stream-only detector), NOT a fair
same-information classifier shoot-out; (b) its continuous score has only 3 levels
(1.0 unmatched / 0.5 finality violation / 0.0 clean), so its AUC-ROC/AUC-PR are COARSE
and not comparable to the continuous scorers — its PRIMARY numbers are the
operating-point recall/precision/phi (per-incident table below), not AUC.

