==========================================================================================
16.5 LOW-AND-SLOW COMPLEMENT (H1) — sequential detector catches what the value z-score misses
==========================================================================================
A. Canonical injected drain (ILLUSTRATIVE — synthetic schedule on real benign cadence):
   per-tx value      = 33,439.74 USD  (1.50 log-sigma above benign)
   benign |log-z| p99 = 4.293  -> the log-scaled value threshold MISSES each tx
   monitor.anomaly.compute_score (the REAL repo value-threshold baseline) on this tx = -0.5781 -> NORMAL (MISSES)
   sequential detector trips after 4 events on the same-counterparty drain
   recall @ operating point:  sequential=0.950   value-zscore=0.000
   => low-and-slow recall gap (detector - z-score) = +0.950  <-- the H1 result
   (B2a: evaluation geometry sigma=1.5/60 events is DISJOINT from the
   selection family sigma=(1.0, 2.0) x events=(30, 90) — header note.)

A'. SIGMA SWEEP (B2b) — same drain schedule at per-tx magnitudes from below to ABOVE
    the value threshold, so the seq-vs-value crossover is SHOWN, not a single point
    where the value baseline is 0-recall by construction:
    value-zscore threshold (TRAIN-matched, 1% budget) = 2.873 log-sigma
     sigma     per-tx USD  seq recall  zscore recall  seq 1st trip note
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
      1.00          9,540       0.917          0.000       event 6 
      1.50         33,440       0.950          0.000       event 4 <- eval point (case A)
      2.00        117,211       0.967          0.000       event 3 
      2.50        410,838       0.967          0.000       event 3 
      2.75        769,170       0.967          0.000       event 3 
      3.00      1,440,039       0.967          1.000       event 3 <- first level above value threshold
      3.50      5,047,519       1.000          1.000       event 1 
      4.00     17,692,196       1.000          1.000       event 1 
    crossover (smallest swept sigma with nonzero value-baseline recall): sigma = 3
    READ: below its threshold the value baseline is blind BY CONSTRUCTION (0 recall);
    above it, each tx flags on its own and both detectors catch the drain. The
    sequential detector's contribution is the sub-threshold band, where accumulation
    is the ONLY signal — the value baseline matching it above the crossover is
    expected and reported, not hidden. Figure: figures/lowslow_sweep.png

B. HONEST CONTRAST — real Nomad structuring subset (destinations with >=3 incident txs):
   structuring destinations = 16; dust fraction (< 1 USD rows) = 0.00
   In the common USD denomination this subset is repeated mid-6-figure transfers to a
   few payees, NOT a pure low-and-slow upward drain. Reported
   transparently — the H1 claim rests on the canonical case (A), not on this subset.
   95% CIs are CLUSTER bootstraps (1000 resamples) over the structuring DESTINATIONS
   (rows within one payee are not independent — the destination is the resampling unit).
   per-tx recall:        sequential=0.633 [0.521,0.709]   value-zscore=0.245 [0.194,0.286]
   per-destination recall: sequential=0.875   value-zscore=0.812

C. DUST SUB-COHORT (real Nomad, value-invisible rows) — structuring rows with value
   < 1 USD in the COMMON USD denomination:
   n_dust_rows = 0 (0% of the structuring subset).
   UNITS-FIX NOTE: the earlier '88% dust' cohort was a mixed-token-decimals ARTIFACT
   (a blanket /1e18 read ~$200 six-decimal stablecoin transfers as ~2e-7 'ETH');
   scored in the common USD denomination the cohort is re-derived from scratch.
   observed: the dust sub-cohort is EMPTY under the corrected units — no structuring
   row is genuinely below the per-tx dust cutoff, so this cohort contributes no
   evidence in either direction; the H1 complement claim rests on the canonical
   injected case (A), carried with its ILLUSTRATIVE label.

