====================================================================================================
No bare inequalities: every comparison below carries a test statistic, a p-value
and (for recall) a confidence interval, via monitor/stats_tests.py.

DeLong correlated-AUC tests on the FULL held-out TEST split (pooled per-row scores),
sequential-cpd (A) vs each baseline (B):
  comparison                                AUC_A    AUC_B      dAUC        z           p
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  seq vs value-zscore                      0.9700   0.8716   +0.0984   +16.64    3.51e-62
  seq vs iforest-corrected                 0.9700   0.7822   +0.1878   +29.06   1.13e-185
  seq vs robust-mad                        0.9700   0.9214   +0.0486    +9.45    3.33e-21
  seq vs xchainwatcher-rules               0.9700   1.0000   -0.0300    -7.51    5.74e-14

READ THIS CORRECTLY (direction computed from this run's DeLong rows):
  pooled per-row AUC significantly HIGHER than: value-zscore, iforest-corrected, robust-mad (dAUC > 0, p < 0.05)
  pooled per-row AUC significantly LOWER than: xchainwatcher-rules (dAUC < 0, p < 0.05)
Neither direction is a claim: per the reframed thesis (V2 2.1) the sequential
detector is NOT sold as a better per-row population classifier — its contribution is
the low-and-slow COMPLEMENT (McNemar below) at a near-zero benign flag rate phi.
The DeLong rows are reported for statistical transparency only.

